---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-rickets`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Rickets',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Rickets', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55.0', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Rickets', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Rickets: The Original Deficiency Disease, Explained"
  description="Nutritional rickets is the childhood bone disease caused by vitamin D or calcium deficiency. How it develops, the classic signs, who is at risk today, and the doses that prevent and treat it."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-rickets", label: "Vitamin D & Rickets" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Rickets</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Rickets is the disease that put vitamin D on the map. It is a failure of the growing skeleton
      to mineralise — soft, weak bone laid down at the growth plates — and in the industrial cities
      of the 19th century it affected the majority of children. Cod liver oil and, later, the
      discovery of vitamin D nearly eliminated it. It has not disappeared, and the children who get
      it today are almost always the ones nobody was supplementing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">What actually goes wrong</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Bone grows at the epiphyseal growth plates, where cartilage is progressively replaced by
      mineralised bone. That mineralisation needs adequate calcium and phosphate at the site — and
      vitamin D is the hormone that keeps blood calcium and phosphate high enough to supply it. When
      25(OH)D falls low enough, calcium absorption drops, parathyroid hormone rises to defend blood
      calcium, and phosphate is dumped in the urine. The growth plate is left without the raw
      material to harden, so it widens and the newly formed bone stays soft (osteoid). The result is
      bowing, swelling, and fractures under a child's own body weight.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The classic signs</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Bowed legs (genu varum)</strong> or knock-knees once the child begins to bear weight.</li>
      <li><strong>Widened wrists and ankles</strong> — palpable swelling at the growth plates.</li>
      <li><strong>Rachitic rosary</strong> — bead-like swellings where ribs meet cartilage.</li>
      <li><strong>Delayed closure of the fontanelle</strong>, soft skull bones (craniotabes), and delayed teeth.</li>
      <li><strong>Delayed walking, muscle weakness, irritability</strong>, and in severe cases hypocalcaemic seizures.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who gets it today</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Nutritional rickets in high-income countries clusters in a predictable group: exclusively
      breastfed infants who are not given a vitamin D supplement (breast milk is low in vitamin D),
      children with darker skin at high latitudes, those with limited sun exposure for cultural or
      medical reasons, and children on restrictive diets. Calcium-deficiency rickets — with adequate
      vitamin D but very low dietary calcium — is a major cause in parts of Africa and Asia. See our
      pages on <a href="/vitamin-d-for-infants" class="text-amber-700 underline">infants</a> and
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-skin-color" class="text-amber-700 underline">skin colour and synthesis</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Prevention</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Prevention is cheap and highly effective. Major paediatric bodies recommend
      <strong>400 IU/day of vitamin D from birth</strong> for all breastfed and partially breastfed
      infants, continued until they are taking at least ~1,000 mL/day of fortified formula. The
      2016 global consensus on nutritional rickets endorses 400 IU/day in the first year and
      600 IU/day thereafter, alongside adequate dietary calcium (~500 mg/day in young children).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Treatment</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Established rickets is treated with therapeutic vitamin D — commonly 2,000 IU/day for
      6–12 weeks, or a single high-dose "stoss" regimen, always with adequate calcium — followed by
      a maintenance dose to prevent recurrence. Radiographs and biochemistry (25(OH)D, calcium,
      phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, PTH) guide the response. Healing is usually complete, though
      severe bowing may need orthopaedic follow-up. Not all rickets is nutritional: hypophosphataemic
      (X-linked) and vitamin D-dependent rickets are genetic and need specialist care, so a
      persistent case despite adequate vitamin D warrants investigation.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Suspected rickets — bowing legs, a swollen wrist, delayed
      walking, or a hypocalcaemic seizure in an infant — needs prompt paediatric assessment, not
      self-treatment. Doses here are illustrative; a clinician should confirm the diagnosis and
      supervise treatment.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-rickets" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
